// test_regalloc_intervals.bpp — Stage C verification fixture (RegAlloc
// v2). Functions named __rg_ivl_* trigger bpp_regalloc.bsm's interval
// debug dump at compile time (regalloc_name_wants_ivl_dump) — this
// file's PURPOSE is the compiler's own stdout while compiling it,
// captured and checked by tests/test_regalloc_intervals.sh.
//
// Same three shapes as test_regalloc_liveness.bpp (Stage B) — their
// use/def/live_in/live_out were already hand-traced and verified
// there. Positions are now STATEMENT-level (a monotonic counter that
// advances once per statement — including T_DECL, which is itself a
// statement with no use/def, but still occupies a position), not one
// per block — and a variable can now own MULTIPLE LiveInterval entries
// (live-range splitting: see regalloc_compute_intervals's own comment)
// when its touches have a genuine gap. The expected numbers below are
// hand-derived by walking each function's backward-then-forward pass
// statement by statement, not guessed.

// vi: a=0, b=1, x=2, y=3. Single block, 4 statements: T_DECL[0],
// x=a+1[1], y=b+x[2], return y[3]. a is read only by statement 1, so
// it's touched at positions {0(carried via live_after),1} -> [0,1].
// b is read by statement 2 and carried through 1 -> touched at
// {0,1,2} -> [0,2]. x is defined at 1, read at 2 -> {1,2} -> [1,2].
// y is defined at 2, read at 3 -> {2,3} -> [2,3]. No gaps anywhere in
// this single straight-line block, so each variable gets exactly one
// interval.
__rg_ivl_straightline(a, b) -> word {
    auto x, y;
    x = a + 1;
    y = b + x;
    return y;
}

// vi: n=0, i=1, sum=2. RPO = [0,1,3,2,4] (entry, header, exit, body,
// continue), 7 statements total: block0=[T_DECL,i=0,sum=0] (positions
// 0-2), block1=[cond i<n] (position 3), block3=[return sum] (position
// 4), block2=[sum=sum+i,i=i+1] (positions 5-6), block4=[] (empty, 0
// statements). The exit block (position 4) sits BETWEEN the header
// and the body in this RPO order (a mutually-exclusive sibling, not a
// predecessor/successor of either) — it doesn't read n or i, so both
// of them show a GAP exactly at position 4 and split into two
// intervals: n -> [0,3] and [5,6]; i -> [1,3] and [5,6] (i isn't
// touched at position 0, the T_DECL). sum IS read at the exit
// (position 4), so it stays one contiguous interval: [2,6].
__rg_ivl_loop(n) -> word {
    auto i, sum;
    i = 0;
    sum = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        sum = sum + i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return sum;
}

// vi: a=0, p=1, q=2. RPO = [0,3,1] (cond, else/join-content, if-arm —
// the if-arm's own unreachable tail, block 2, is correctly absent). 6
// statements: block0=[T_DECL,cond a>0] (positions 0-1), block3=[q=a-1,
// return q] (positions 2-3), block1=[p=a+1,return p] (positions 4-5).
// `a` is read at position 1 (the cond) and again at position 2
// (q=a-1) and position 4 (p=a+1) — but NOT at position 3 (return q,
// the if-arm's mutually-exclusive sibling), so it splits: [0,2] and
// [4,4]. `p` is defined at 4, read at 5 -> [4,5]. `q` is defined at 2,
// read at 3 -> [2,3]. p and q's intervals do NOT overlap — the exact
// non-overlapping-live-range property this whole arc exists to let a
// future stage (linear-scan) notice and share a register for.
__rg_ivl_nonoverlap(a) -> word {
    auto p, q;
    if (a > 0) {
        p = a + 1;
        return p;
    }
    q = a - 1;
    return q;
}

main() {
    auto r;
    r = __rg_ivl_straightline(10, 20);
    if (r != 31) { return 1; }
    r = __rg_ivl_loop(5);
    if (r != 10) { return 2; }
    r = __rg_ivl_nonoverlap(5);
    if (r != 6) { return 3; }
    r = __rg_ivl_nonoverlap(0 - 5);
    if (r != 0 - 6) { return 4; }
    return 0;
}
